$DIR/main.p:9:9: cannot return strings.Slice(unsafe.String(&b[0], 2), 0, 1), which points into b, a local array of f
    strings.Slice(unsafe.String(&b[0], 2), 0, 1) points into b. b is a local
    array of f (declared at main.p:8). Its memory is reclaimed when f returns,
    so strings.Slice(unsafe.String(&b[0], 2), 0, 1) would point at memory that
    no longer holds b.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return values instead of views of b, or allocate b and return its owner, an
    owned[[2]byte]:
        b := new([2]byte)
        ...
        return b
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
